task: bench-refresh
agent: opus5-bench
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/bench-refresh
worktree: ../okay-wt-bench

why: the operator asked for the comparison benchmarks to be re-run and
  every number in the documentation refreshed. docs/benchmarks.md's own
  rule is that lanes quoted together were measured in the same session;
  its tables have accumulated across a dozen sessions on hosts of
  varying quiet. One session, one host, one protocol makes the tables
  comparable to each other again.

what: re-measure every CROSS-LIBRARY lane (the ones that quote
  cats-effect / ZIO / kyo / fs2 / atnos / circe) — CompareBenchmark,
  DirectBenchmark, Reader, Writer, Choice, Async, StreamOps, Merge,
  ChunkFlush, IdiomaticApi, Resource, Generator, Text, Codec, Rag, and
  FairnessProbe's floor lanes — in one session on the freshly-booted
  quiet box, append every row to src/jmh/history.tsv, and write the new
  numbers into docs/benchmarks.md sections 0-11 with the run's date and
  protocol stated. Scope chosen by the operator over "everything in
  compare/" and over "headline only".

predict: most rows land within noise of the current table. The box is
  quiet and freshly booted where several of the standing rows were
  measured "box shared with sibling builds all day", so the honest
  expectation is that MANY rows move DOWN together — which is a host
  effect, not a win, and the doc must say so rather than claim
  improvement. Orderings between libraries should not change; if one
  does, that row gets re-run before it is written.

CAUTION — MACHINE STATE THIS CLAIM OWNS:
  both scalascript launchd guards are UNLOADED for the duration
  (operator's decision, 2026-09-07):
    launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.scalascript.build-ram-guard.plist
    launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.scalascript.kill-stale-builders.plist
  If this session dies, whoever finds this claim must load them back.

STATUS 2026-09-07 22:35 — PAUSED, twice over, and both reasons matter
  to whoever resumes this:

  1. The scheduler changed under it. Every round measured before
     3f09bd9c is from a tree with the own-long-join-deadlock defect in
     it. Those rounds are DISCARDED, not merged with post-fix ones —
     the doc's own rule is that lanes quoted together come from one
     session on one tree.
  2. A sibling holds `scala-3-9` (migrating the build to Scala 3.9
     LTS). That changes the compiler generating every byte being
     timed, so a table measured on 3.7.4 and published while master is
     on 3.9 describes a tree nobody has. Asked in the room who goes
     first; my preference is 3.9 lands, then I measure on top of it,
     so the numbers match master and it costs one clean session
     instead of two.

  Resuming needs: a QUIET box (no sbt, no sibling build — otherwise
  the numbers are noise), the runner in this session's scratchpad
  (`run-round.sh`, one JVM per benchmark class, `-f 1`, each class at
  its own declared @Warmup/@Measurement, 3 rounds, per-lane minimum),
  and a fresh `compare/Jmh/compile` + classpath export.

  Known before it restarts:
  - `DirectBenchmark` is in package `okaybench`, not `okay`; the class
    pattern must be `.*\.<Class>\..*`.
  - `CodecBenchmark.scala:192` warns on master (E176, an unused
    Boolean from `RuntimeStaged.install()`). Pre-existing, out of this
    lane's scope, belongs in BACKLOG.
  - §5 and §10 carry B/op columns; they need a `-prof gc` pass.

STATUS 2026-09-07 23:02 — RESUMED, on the operator's instruction, and
  BOTH blockers are gone:
  - the scheduler fix landed (3f09bd9c), so the tree under measurement
    no longer loses tasks;
  - `scala-3-9` LANDED (75377969, master cf820fa7). The build is on
    Scala 3.9.0 LTS, so this session measures the compiler that master
    actually uses, and the doc will say "Scala 3.9.0".
  Every round taken before this point is discarded. Nothing measured
